9. Source Control Strategy

9.1 Branches

main: stable and reviewed code.

develop: integration branch if the team continues using it.

feature/<short-name>: feature development.

fix/<short-name>: defect correction.

docs/<short-name>: documentation changes.

9.2 Workflow

git pull origin develop
git checkout -b feature/short-name

After implementation and local testing:

git add <specific-files>
git commit -m "Add concise feature description"
git push origin feature/short-name

Create a pull request from the feature branch to the integration branch. Merge only after required checks and review are complete.

9.3 Pull Request Requirements

Every pull request should include:

Problem and implemented solution.

Scope and files changed.

Acceptance criteria addressed.

Screenshots for visual changes.

API examples for endpoint changes.

Database migration notes.

Tests executed and results.

Known limitations or follow-up issues.

9.4 Pull Request Checklist

The issue and acceptance criteria are linked.

The branch contains only relevant changes.

Code formatting and linting pass.

Tests pass or limitations are documented.

Database migrations are included when required.

API documentation is updated.

UI screenshots are included when applicable.

Security and authorization effects are reviewed.

Creator rights and licence effects are reviewed when applicable.

At least one peer review is complete.

